Fubini's theorem demonstrates that under certain assumptions the double integral over a rectangle (i.e., the limit of Riemann sums) can be calculated by using iterated intergrals and, moreover, that the order of integration for the iterated integral does not matter. 
We remark that the independence of the order of integration depends strongly on the fact that the region of integration is rectangular; it will not generalize to more arbitrary regions in such a simple way. 

